---
import Icon from '../Icon.astro';
import SectionHead from './SectionHead.astro';
---

<section id="developers" class="band" aria-labelledby="developers-title">
  <SectionHead id="developers-title" eyebrow="For developers" title="Fast because of how it's built, not because we said so.">
    <p>
      You've read enough landing pages that promise speed with a chart and no method. Here's the architecture instead,
      and you can check every line of it.
    </p>
  </SectionHead>
  <div class="cards">
    <article>
      <Icon name="FiZap" size={22} class="icon" />
      <h3>Speed you can reason about</h3>
      <p>
        One Rust binary on Axum and Tokio, and Postgres beside it. That's the whole install. Each document sits in a
        single row, its published fields in one JSONB column. A post's own fields come straight from that row;
        linked documents you ask to populate come from theirs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Numbers? Not yet. We'll publish reproducible benchmarks against the popular CMSs first, method and all, and
        only then quote one.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <Icon name="FiLock" size={22} class="icon" />
      <h3>Security that lives in the query</h3>
      <p>
        An access rule isn't a check bolted on after the fetch. It becomes part of the SQL for whoever's asking, so
        nobody can find a hidden document by filtering for it. Filter values always travel as bound parameters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Passwords are hashed with Argon2id, refused if they've turned up in a known breach, and five wrong ones in 15
        minutes lock the account for 15 more. API keys carry scopes and can be rotated.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <Icon name="FiTool" size={22} class="icon" />
      <h3>Built to be lived with</h3>
      <p>
        When a write fails, the error names the field: <code>seo.title</code>, <code>links.0.url</code>. Every error is
        RFC 9457 problem+json with a request ID you'll find in the logs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Content types export to a JSON file you can diff in a pull request. IDs are UUIDv7, deletes are soft, and
        date-times are stored in UTC to the millisecond.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="more">
    <a href="https://docs.tachyoncms.com/concepts">How TachyonCMS fits together <Icon name="FiArrowRight" size={16} /></a>
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  /* Three columns under one line, split by thin rules: no boxes. */
  .cards {
    display: grid;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-line);
  }
  article {
    padding: 32px 0;
  }
  article + article {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-line);
  }
  article :global(.icon) {
    color: var(--c-blue);
  }
  h3 {
    margin: 18px 0 12px;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  article p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--c-muted);
  }
  article p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  code {
    font: 400 0.875em var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--c-text);
  }
  .more {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
  }
  .more a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .more a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
    article {
      padding: 36px 32px 40px;
    }
    article:first-child {
      padding-left: 0;
    }
    article:last-child {
      padding-right: 0;
    }
    article + article {
      border-top: 0;
      border-left: 1px solid var(--c-line);
    }
  }
</style>
